{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q006_ubiquitin_like_function_divergence","answer":"SCOPe supports divergent functions within a shared evolutionary scaffold. In release 2.08, Ubiquitin-related (d.15.1.1; SUNID 54237), UBX domain (d.15.1.2; 54250), and Ras-binding domain, RBD (d.15.1.5; 54263) are separate families within the Ubiquitin-like superfamily (d.15.1; 54236). They share the beta-Grasp (ubiquitin-like) fold (d.15; 54235), in the Alpha and beta proteins (a+b) class. Their shared superfamily supports probable common ancestry, beyond merely sharing a fold; family membership denotes closer evolutionary relationships. Fold or class membership alone would not establish homology.

Biologically, this connects ubiquitin-related modifiers and interaction domains: ubiquitin can be covalently attached to substrates, many UBX domains recruit the p97/VCP/Cdc48 ATPase, and RBDs recognize Ras-family GTPases in signaling. The shared scaffold is useful for structural comparison and investigating how binding surfaces and functions diverged.

It would be unsafe to transfer ubiquitin conjugation, proteasomal degradation tagging, p97 binding, or Ras binding across these families solely from their superfamily assignment or structural resemblance. Even ubiquitin-related family membership does not guarantee a conjugatable modifier or a degradation signal. Specific functional annotation requires sequence and interface conservation, appropriate terminal features and domain context, and biochemical evidence of binding or conjugation. A cross-family structural template may inform the conserved core but cannot establish partner specificity or interchangeable function.","evidence_used":["Pretrained model knowledge of SCOPe hierarchy and ubiquitin, UBX, and RBD biology","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ubiquitin&mode=auto&limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15.1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15.1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.15.1/children?limit=100"]}]}
